steps.body:
Lists can insert, remove, and search anywhere:

- `list.add(i, x)` inserts `x` at index `i` and shifts the rest right.
- `list.remove(i)` removes the item at index `i` and returns it.
- `list.contains(x)` is `true` when `x` is in the list.
- `list.indexOf(x)` is the index of the first match, or `-1` when missing.
- `list.isEmpty()` is `true` when the size is 0.
- `list.clear()` removes everything.

Searching uses `equals`, so it compares values, not references.

On a `List<String>`, `remove("Ann")` removes the first `"Ann"` by value. A `List<Integer>` has a trap: `remove(30)` means **index** 30, not the value 30, because an `int` argument picks the index version. Pass an `Integer` object to remove by value:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<Integer> ids = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30, 40));
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(ids);
    }
}
```

```java
ids.remove(1);                   // removes the item at index 1
ids.remove(Integer.valueOf(30)); // removes the value 30
```

